id":"msg_0e4791b78803f84b016aceb4efa06887d2a203ca12682eb9d9","type":"message","status":"completed","content":[{"type":"output_text","annotations":[],"logprobs":[],"text":"**Yes.** Put \\(F=K(i)\\). We use the standard properties of a real-closed field: \\(K\\) is ordered, every positive element is a square, and every odd-degree polynomial has a root in \\(K\\). In particular, \\(\\operatorname{char}K=0\\) and \\([F:K]=2\\).\n\nFirst, every element of \\(F\\) is a square. For \\(a+bi\\) with \\(b\\ne0\\), set\n\\[\nr=\\sqrt{a^2+b^2},\\qquad\nu=\\sqrt{\\frac{r+a}{2}},\\qquad v=\\frac{b}{2u}.\n\\]\nHere \\(r>|a|\\), so \\(u>0\\), and direct calculation gives\n\\[\n(u+vi)^2=a+bi.\n\\]\nWhen \\(b=0\\), use \\(\\sqrt a\\) if \\(a\\ge0\\), and \\(i\\sqrt{-a}\\) otherwise. Consequently, \\(F\\) has no quadratic extension, by the quadratic formula.\n\nNow let \\(f\\in F[x]\\) be nonconstant. Choose a finite Galois extension \\(L/K\\) containing \\(F\\) and all roots of \\(f\\): take the splitting field over \\(K\\) of \\(x^2+1\\), \\(f\\), and its coefficientwise conjugate. Write \\(G=\\operatorname{Gal}(L/K)\\), and let \\(P\\) be a Sylow \\(2\\)-subgroup. Then\n\\[\n[L^P:K]=[G:P]\n\\]\nis odd. A nontrivial finite odd-degree extension of \\(K\\) is impossible: by the primitive element theorem, it would yield an irreducible odd-degree polynomial of degree greater than one, contradicting the root property. Thus \\(L^P=K\\), so \\(G=P\\) is a \\(2\\)-group.\n\nIf \\(L\\ne F\\), the nontrivial \\(2\\)-group \\(\\operatorname{Gal}(L/F)\\) has a subgroup of index \\(2\\). Its fixed field would be a quadratic extension of \\(F\\), a contradiction. Hence \\(L=F\\), and \\(f\\) splits over \\(F\\).\n\nThus \\(F\\) is algebraically closed and algebraic over \\(K\\).\n\nFinal answer: \\(K(i)\\) is an algebraic closure of \\(K\\)."}],"phase":"final_answer","role":"assistant"}],"parallel_tool_calls":true,"presence_penalty":0,"previous_response_id":null,"prompt_cache_key":null,"prompt_ca